**Signature (Public client harness)** · `sig:design:client` pn-client accepts CBOR-native GraphQL and returns GraphQL, using the node pn's schema as an unchanged subset plus client workflow roots (`dec:harness:client-schema`). Its one byte execution entry point is an embedding boundary, not a parallel Rust API for faucet, burn, submit or read. Relayed node operations preserve node outcomes, errors and exact canonical document bytes; pn-interchange supplies strict document decoding and bounded message reading on both sides. Client workflows cover faucet funding, burn-new-account, burn-existing-account and recovery; each has explicit stages, independent local limits and uncertain-effect outcomes under (`rule:api:limits`), (`rule:api:outcomes`). pn-wallet owns wallet funding and all spend construction, signing, broadcast and recovery via Elements RPC, with node funding calls transported as GraphQL by pn-client through an injected channel. The client asks pn-wallet for the transaction already associated with the request before any new spend; no client PostgreSQL or local journal exists (`rule:harness:wallet-recovery`). Validate the run and authenticated permanent target before irreversible work. Existing spendable tokens may fund another account's fresh burn, but old burn amount never moves. Private custody and full public observation follow (`rec:observe:decision`). Package roles and directions are decided by (`dec:harness:client-packages`); added placement is accepted under (`proposal:harness:packages`), while implementation evidence remains held.

**Signature (Development wallet)** · `sig:design:wallet` pn-wallet owns the test-asset workflow and its state in the Elements wallet. For each run/request, retain the owned inputs of every signed or possibly signed attempt and require their common intersection to remain nonempty before signing under (`rule:harness:wallet-recovery`). Attempts may differ in amount, fee, outputs and inputs; consensus permits at most one to confirm. Validate the signed permanent-target and request commitment, serialize selection across writers and recover descriptor spends from chain and pool. Funding chooses a receiving destination and preserves the node grant request; a lost grant reply is resolved against node status and wallet receipt, not replaced by another grant. Wallet comments and transaction summaries are not independent burn evidence. An incomplete scan, locked/unavailable wallet, missing spent-output notes or ambiguous conflict produces unavailable or incomplete with unknown effect; never sign a disjoint retry or claim rollback. Persist prepared account binding with the node request or recover its public receipt, so a lost reply cannot silently create another account. Production spending still owes crash-durable all-writer input admission and complete bounded descriptor/chain recovery; the finite PSET witnesses do not discharge that integration hold. Node quota/history storage remains separate under (`rule:devnet:history`). This is the sole Elements RPC boundary for pn-host and pn-client, covering chain/profile inspection, raw burn evidence, settlement checks and faucet spending as well as client burns. Host retains quota/history transactions; pn-wallet owns descriptor signing and recovery while consensus selects the confirmed attempt. Its chain slice lands in bite 2 and its spending/client workflows in bite 8 under (`amend:decomposition:infrastructure`). Admit the integration once with secret-preserving state/errors, while each caller retains its own authorization and independent conformance obligations. The first slice uses cookie-authenticated bounded HTTP through the secret-owning transport and the evaluated exact decoder (`amend:devnet:wallet-cookie`), (`amend:devnet:rpc-bounds`), (`amend:devnet:wallet-decoder`). It reads chain evidence only; later signing is delegated to the Elements wallet, with local cryptographic operation admission governed by (`amend:crypto:wallet-operation-scope`) and (`amend:crypto:wallet-codec-features`). Its read-only cookie is not a restricted server identity; decide that identity before adding spending methods (`open:devnet:spending-rpc-identity`).

