## Resources and performance · `sec:design:resources`

**Experiment (Declared workloads)** · `expt:design:workloads` Each implementation bite declares workloads by anchored Actors, accepted acts, complete projections, dependency incidence, sparse base nonzeros, supported horizon, integer bit length, precision and raw path-prefix count. Include isolated Actors, dense support, shared dependency ancestors, equal-width differently shaped graphs, depleted relays and adversarial signed cycles. Record input construction separately from solve, publication and replay; no measured throughput or deployment capacity is claimed here. Observation follows (`rec:observe:decision`). Each workload receives a benchmark case, declared measurement configuration and retained results under (`rule:harness:benchmarks`).

**Requirement (Limit declaration)** · `req:design:limits` A supported execution profile supplies finite limits for input bytes, object count, references, nesting, rational bits, matrix support, horizon, interval precision, primitive operations, iterations, witness bytes and raw prefixes. Preflight excess reports unsupported; exhaustion during certified refinement reports INDECISIVE with its counter, while incomplete structural enumeration reports resource exhaustion without a mathematical verdict. A host's local deadline cannot rewrite the published certification policy. Release support follows (`rule:compatibility:pre-one`). Observation follows (`rec:observe:decision`).

**Rule (Cost accounting)** · `rule:design:costs` Sparse support propagation charges work in horizon times base nonzeros, including construction of contributing extrema and retained diagonals; fence evaluation is then a separate scalar cost. A map application charges sparse hop products and precision-dependent arithmetic. Factorized publication avoids a dense expansion but must retain the complete emitted operator. Raw enumeration charges visited-set work and every depth-dependent prefix, including parallel projections; it has no general constant-factor traversal claim (``prop:scalability:standing-certificate-cost``, ``lem:epoch:double-cover-bfs-correctness``). Observation follows (`rec:observe:decision`).

**Experiment (Measurements before feasibility)** · `expt:design:measurement-gate` Before commissioning a workload profile, measure peak memory, wall time, operation counts, precision growth, evidence size and rejection behavior on the declared workload family. Record compiler, backend, platform and resource limits with the measurements. The performance risk (`warn:vision:performance`) remains held for feasibility until those measurements support a deployment budget; the semantic response to exhaustion is fixed here. Observation follows (`rec:observe:decision`). Comparable baseline and threshold declarations determine warnings and regression findings under (`rule:harness:benchmarks`), (`rule:devperf:comparison`); fast smoke runs cannot establish feasibility, and timing never fails a test, tier or gate.

## Development performance · `sec:design:development-performance`

**Rule (Bite timing budgets)** · `rule:design:development-budgets` Apply (`tab:devperf:budgets`) from the foundation slice and to every subsequent bite: unit, public-surface, integration, conformance and property cases retain their own scopes; named suites select the pure, mock or concrete real-service row. The suite register (`model:design:harness-suites`) and claim ledger (`ledger:design:claim-plan`) supply the level, implementation and workload association, never an elapsed-time verdict. {{ require(row('org.peer-network.devperf.budgets', name='gate').budget_ms > 0 and row('org.peer-network.devperf.budgets', name='service-gate').budget_ms > 0, 'both gate paths need explicit budgets') }}Longer prebuilt cases select the extended invocation by the declared allowance threshold in (`rule:harness:tiers`), keeping the complete required result union. The fast author-tier budget inside the per-change gate is {{ row('org.peer-network.devperf.budgets', name='gate').number_forms.budget_ms | display }} milliseconds. Case budgets are judged in the separate calibration invocation (`conv:design:case-budget-calibration`); the concurrent gate's BUDGET-LEDGER is informational, not a verdict. The complete verdict waits for every required tier and their evidence join, so the whole critical path has its own {{ row('org.peer-network.devperf.budgets', name='whole-gate').number_forms.budget_ms | display }} millisecond warning bound. GATE-WALL reports that inclusive interval from structural preparation after target-lock acquisition through all required execution, joins and resource collection, with own CPU, pressure and the same overrun classification as a tier. Lock waiting is reported separately. A green comparison retains the whole interval with its tier baseline, and unavailable prior whole measurements stay unclassified on overrun. Required tiers that cannot fit that path run alongside it as their own invocations: the reference-certificate budget is {{ row('org.peer-network.devperf.budgets', name='reference-gate').number_forms.budget_ms | display }} milliseconds and the real-service conformance budget, including Elements and PostgreSQL, is {{ row('org.peer-network.devperf.budgets', name='service-gate').number_forms.budget_ms | display }} milliseconds. Import requires every tier green and the complete evidence join; concurrent waiting drops no evidence. The required extended invocation has a budget of {{ row('org.peer-network.devperf.budgets', name='extended-gate').number_forms.budget_ms | display }} milliseconds. It owns the design-checker mutation suite, observation example build, runtime observation-mode matrix and parser witnesses. These complete refusal and cross-process matrices retain their existing case and suite budgets; moving their invocation restores margin for compile, lint and core test feedback without weakening any assertion. The scheduler retains every dependency and the result union admits each required case exactly once. The per-change budget remains the design target, rather than being enlarged to fit an outgrown workload. A functionally green import proceeds when its author tier or whole critical path exceeds its wall budget, with the excess recorded as a finding separately from the verdict. The budget keeps author feedback fast: a green change need not wait for a busy node, while growth in its own work has an owner who must answer for it. Classify an overrun in order: unclassified if the baseline wall or either pressure fraction is unavailable, then high-stall for a matching executed case set when current wall multiplied by its pressure fraction covers the entire budget excess and the current wall does not exceed the baseline wall multiplied by the baseline non-stalled fraction and divided by the current non-stalled fraction, then rebuild when changed compile inputs accompany positive serialized compile-step wall growth and comparable compile CPU grows by more than the declared CPU margin, provided that growth either covers the wall excess or the comparable non-compile CPU remains within that margin. The non-compile comparison subtracts metered comparable compilation from both sides, so an isolated rebuild is not charged as growth in the program it builds. This alternative requires comparable case CPU and nonnegative residual CPU on both sides; it does not add parallel compile wall to a serial path. Without comparable case CPU, the remaining classification is unavailable. Otherwise classify load-unresolved when pressure exceeds {{ row('org.peer-network.devperf.budgets', name='gate').number_forms.load_unresolved_pressure_percent | display }} percent, because contention can also inflate CPU and requires a quiet re-measurement before attributing the remaining excess, then plain unless comparable own process-and-child CPU, less comparable compile CPU on both sides when compile inputs changed and that subtraction is available, exceeds the comparison by more than {{ row('org.peer-network.devperf.budgets', name='gate').number_forms.cpu_growth_percent | display }} percent. Baselines are retained by source tree within the same target and declared lineage; an explicit import base selects its matching tree, so a green but unimported candidate cannot erase the comparison. Without an import base, the latest retained ancestor supplies the comparison, with identical harness configuration, tier roster and jobs, compiler identity, runtime configuration and Cargo configuration. PN_GATE_LINEAGE names main for the import loop; lane invocations default to their own checkout identity. A comparison source must be an ancestor of the current source, or its recorded tree must equal the validated import base tree. Re-signing an imported commit preserves that tree, so successive imports retain a comparison without admitting an unrelated tree. The gate records the source, tree, matching relation and CPU beside the current measurement. A baseline that already exceeds its budget cannot make an overrun with no stall a high-stall finding. The high-stall explanation is continuous: an overrun that the measured stall share alone explains is not plain growth. A pressure fraction outside the measurable interval leaves the comparison unclassified. This warning comparison does not establish a benchmark regression. Tier and whole-gate performance warnings carry the same classes as their corresponding summary lines. Each green baseline retains individual case and obligation statuses; the next comparable gate names every changed status, including added or removed evidence, rather than only printing totals. Re-measure a high-stall overrun quietly before attributing its cause. Rebuild inputs are changed tracked paths under packages/ or vendor/, package manifests and the dependency lock. Sum wall growth only on the declared serialized compilation steps; parallel isolated compilation cannot explain that path by adding CPU. Isolated owners meter compile-only Cargo commands and their waited descendants separately. The fixed-point invocation builds the documents executable first and then runs it directly, excluding rendering from compile CPU. Mixed Cargo-and-run commands remain explicitly reported apart from compile CPU; their program work cannot establish a rebuild. Comparable compile CPU includes metered isolated compilation, excluding the same cold or changed-layout steps on both sides, without treating parallel wall as serialized growth. Every step retains its own figures and isolation layout identity. An isolated owner starting without its retained target, or changing its storage/configuration identity, marks its step partial in the published baseline; those steps, and steps whose storage or configuration key differs, are excluded from the next comparison while the rest remain comparable. The tier line names the excluded steps, comparable CPU and serialized compile-wall growth. When executed case sets differ, compare their intersection by subtracting each added case's printed own CPU from the current comparable total and each removed case's printed own CPU from the baseline total. This retains a useful work comparison without charging a newly executed service witness as growth in unchanged cases. The baseline retains those per-case costs; the tier line names both differences and both subtractions. Missing, invalid or inconsistent exclusive-case costs, or non-comparable steps whose case costs cannot be attributed, withhold aggregate comparison. Unchanged sets need no historical per-case costs. Full wall remains the cost of the verdict: overlapping case durations cannot be subtracted from a concurrent critical path, so high-stall wall attribution still requires identical executed sets. Intersection comparisons can classify plain, growth, load-unresolved or independently supported rebuild work. The whole comparison applies the same rule across every tier. The package-wide change filter conservatively covers generated carriers, embedded data and build-script inputs without claiming a complete reconstruction of Cargo dependencies; changed paths alone cannot establish rebuild work: comparable compile CPU must grow and serialized compile wall must grow, with either excess coverage or a stable comparable non-compile remainder; contention can stretch wall without increasing the executed compilation work. Re-measure rebuild work on the next import. Other own-CPU growth goes to the owning lane's budget review before that lane's next import. Repeated overruns prompt review of the tier budget. Independent steps use the declared {{ row('org.peer-network.harness.registry', kind='tier', name='fast').jobs | number }} jobs, with build dependencies and shared-target Cargo serialization retained. Collect inclusive wall time through (`rule:observe:metering`), with current script timers retained until coverage is proven. Use verification gate log artifacts and later operation traces with declared cache state under (`rule:devperf:comparison`); contention remains visible context and never changes a functional result. Resource counters in (`req:design:limits`) still determine supported work and exhaustion, independently of these warning thresholds.

