**Observation (Released finite witnesses)** · `obs:design:released-witnesses` ``ver:can:depth-two-overlap`` checks declared disjoint and shared-leaf terminal cases, while ``ver:tensors:polarization-reversal`` separates stance sign from scalar tensor magnitudes. ``ver:raw:bid-maturity-pair`` compares raw routes and a fixed reward context, not standing allocation. ``ver:tier:bleed-scaling`` checks declared restricted-route scaling, without making it a universal full-route ordering. ``ver:standing:depth-support-comparison`` compares distinct normalized supports with separately certified equilibria; a passing certificate alone does not prove their difference nonzero. ``ver:standing:transport-equivariance`` checks complete-input relabelling, symmetry and deeper-path sensitivity. Bites 4 and 5 cover geometry and complete-act compilation, bite 6 certified standing, bite 10 optional raw service and bite 11 cross-crate evidence. CAN and other terminal consumer checks are deployment-owned reference comparisons outside the implementation scope (`open:inventory:deployment-terminal-evidence`); none of these paper results substitutes for executed test-site claims.

**Open Question (Core standing property evidence)** · `open:design:core-standing-properties` The finite implementation evidence is closed by (`amend:design:bite-six-close`). The remaining hold is unrestricted cross-crate histories, randomized consumer guarantees and unbounded families, owned by the verification implementer and evidence reviewer in bite 11. Universal funded-partition invariance is false: matched complete histories can change export and standing, and concentration can span nearly a whole hull width (``prop:epoch:split-concentration``, ``ver:standing:split-concentration``). Under ``thm:epoch:split-observational-equivalence``, two completions with identical public acts, payloads, ledgers, constants and all rule inputs have identical deterministic outputs, or the same output distributions under the same conditional randomness law. A record-based remedy imposes the same standing or fixed-context terminal change on legitimate separation and strategic concentration. ``prop:epoch:split-legitimate-completion`` establishes a legitimate completion only in its admitted receiving family, with independently assignable private roles, no contradictory aggregate-scope payload claim, donors able to intend the retained role and no extra public identity or purpose attestation. It does not infer actual intent or cover every strategic history. The examples ``ex:epoch:split-small`` and ``ex:epoch:split-showcase`` concern two interpretations of one partitioned record, not equality between pooled and partitioned records. No design rule classifies private purpose or suppresses funded partition as strategic; source and act-debit requirements still apply equally. Universal burn monotonicity remains false under ``thm:epoch:burn-response``; recovery requires ``cor:epoch:burn-monotone-regimes`` or the finite response test. The compiler, certified solver and integrated replay witnesses of (`verif:design:core-standing-properties`) close their finite scope in bites 5 and 6. Bite 11 owns unrestricted families; this is not a paper-maintainer question.

## Risks, reconciliation and open questions · `sec:design:questions`

**Resolution (Design reconciliation)** · `resol:design:reconciliation` (`open:vision:design-reconciliation`) is settled as an explicit compatibility account: the semantic package remains complete, deployment choices remain declared, wall-declaration obligations and remaining controller holds follow the released status, and external encoding follows interchange through the accepted proposals (`proposal:design:encoding`) and (`proposal:design:interchange-dependency`). Record 007 carries their acceptance in (`amend:decomposition:publication`); completion of the byte profile remains the publication-bite obligation (`open:design:publication-profile`). Release support follows (`rule:compatibility:pre-one`).

**Resolution (Evidence coverage)** · `resol:design:coverage` (`open:vision:evidence-coverage`) is settled at the planning level by (`ledger:design:evidence`): every concept requirement has fixtures, properties, an independent-oracle route and a stated hold. Execution coverage remains held until the corresponding bites populate that ledger with actual results; no domain fixture is represented as already passing.

**Resolution (Numerical evidence)** · `resol:design:numerical-evidence` (`open:vision:numerical-evidence`) is settled for the public contract by exact carriers, checked enclosures, residual evidence, equality witnesses and explicit indecision. The concrete admitted backend fragment, its finite adversarial witnesses and supported target are joined at (`open:design:numeric-backend`) and (`amend:design:backend-soundness-dossier`); additional platform and controller boundaries retain their named holds; independent review of the finite scope is joined in (`amend:design:bite-six-close`). The first records bite may establish structural carriers without claiming those nonlinear operations implemented.

**Warning (False confidence)** · `warn:design:false-confidence` The design response to (`warn:vision:false-confidence`) separates completeness, safety and independent evidence in (`rule:design:independence`) and requires act-omission and correlated-error challenges. Residual confidence remains held until those checks execute and their independence is reviewed; a shared-kernel replay match cannot close it. Owner: the verification-bite maintainer and audit reviewer.

**Warning (Performance)** · `warn:design:performance` The response to (`warn:vision:performance`) is the declared workload, limit and measurement contract. Feasibility remains held because no domain implementation has measured those workloads. Owner: each bite's implementer, with the commissioning reviewer accepting the resulting deployment budget; settlement requires (`expt:design:measurement-gate`) without relaxing exact semantics. Observation follows (`rec:observe:decision`).

**Open Question (Proposal acceptance)** · `open:design:proposal-review` {{ require(amendment.status == 'accepted', 'proposal review is settled only by an accepted amendment') }}Settled by the repository owner's acceptance, recorded with its reasons in (`amend:decomposition:publication`), (`dec:decomposition:encoding`) and (`conv:decomposition:dependencies`). Owner: repository maintainers. The encoding replacement and pn-host infrastructure dependency preserve the semantic package, domain boundaries and bite order; no acceptance hold remains for those publication proposals. Storage, API, development adapter and harness placement are also accepted under (`amend:decomposition:infrastructure`); their implementation evidence remains held. The separate profile-completion obligation is (`open:design:publication-profile`). Release support follows (`rule:compatibility:pre-one`).

**Open Question (Deployment instantiation)** · `open:design:deployment-profile` Owner: the deployment. Supply public effective choices for (`model:design:profile`), including the fixed structural floor, published wall controller, comparison procedure and supported numerical policy, then demonstrate their constraints on exact boundary fixtures. Until then the design supports contracts and reference cases but cannot certify a concrete deployment. This preserves (`open:vision:deployment-profile`) and (`open:vision:operational-adoption`). Release support follows (`rule:compatibility:pre-one`). The owned bite-eight settling checklist is (`rec:design:bite-eight-checklist`).

**Open Question (Wall performance and boundary evidence)** · `open:design:wall-performance` Owner: the adopting deployment for floor economics, controller parameters, its published marker policy, published epoch-marker placement rule and cadence, and verified realization evidence under ``cond:deployment:wall-calibration`` and ``cond:deployment:controller-performance``. These are published deployment obligations with declared criteria and measurements, not holds awaiting a paper calibration decision. The common accepted snapshot is explained by ``rem:epoch:shared-burn-snapshot-rationale`` and settled by ``prop:comparator:marker-settlement-consistency`` conditional on recognition, not held for a new consistency theorem. Deployment settlement requires conforming marker evidence and declared economic and plant assumptions with reproducible controller trajectories. No compulsory or timed acceptance, eventual marker production or positive standing follows; controller-performance and liveness claims remain unproved.

**Resolution (Certified bound scope)** · `resol:design:certified-bound-scope` The conserved-map safety argument uses the evaluated hop-profile width fence and accepted-rung uniqueness theorem (``rem:dynamics:certified-bound-scope``). Selected-path circulant extrema supply no upper bound on the conserved map: the sibling response obstructs entrywise domination, and a finite-max envelope theorem does not compare these models (``prop:dynamics:sibling-response-without-path``). The removed coordinate coefficient supplies no implementation obligation. Smooth arguments require a fixed responsive cell; approximate-solver drift does not describe exact epoch equilibria, a zero-tilt equality case is not a bifurcation onset, and failed certification proves neither contraction nor divergence (``subsec:dynamics:quarantined-apparatus-scope``).

**Open Question (Residual normative conflict)** · `open:design:paper-conflict` Owner: the paper's maintainers when a conflicting pair of released claims is identified. Record both labels and premises, hold only the affected claim, and require clarification or a released correction. This escalation route follows (`open:vision:paper-conflict`); historical DEFECT classifications alone do not open new holds after their corrected Status. Any concept or record defect discovered in later implementation is reported for its owner rather than repaired through implementation behavior.

## Design gate · `sec:design:gate`

**Gate (Before the first domain bite)** · `gate:design:domain-entry` First close (`gate:design:observability-foundation`) and (`gate:design:claim-foundation`), then review and accept the concept-to-design coverage, domain contracts, frozen-state model, verdict algebra and scoped holds. Every requirement must have a realizing decision and evidence plan; every known unsupported choice must identify the dependent claim and owner. The first structural records bite may proceed with later numerical and publication evidence held only where its carriers preserve the contracts and make no unsupported claim. Observation follows (`rec:observe:decision`). Also close (`gate:design:harness-foundation`) and (`gate:design:performance-foundation`) so the first crate has its unit, public-surface, integration and conformance evidence routes plus measured budgets and nonfailing performance findings.

**Gate (Dependency readiness)** · `gate:design:dependency-readiness` Before the affected bite, resolve its deployment declaration, numeric backend or publication profile gate and transcribe its independent fixtures. Do not lock a charged-value encoding without its interpretation and certification contract, execute a wall update without its published controller, fixed floor and comparison evidence, or claim external byte compatibility before the assigned theory and vectors exist. These are bite-specific prerequisites, not a reason to disguise unfinished evidence as complete. Release support follows (`rule:compatibility:pre-one`).

**Gate (Document integrity)** · `gate:design:document-integrity` Render all inputs and documents to a fixed point, check every repository citation and environment kind, and pass the repository gate under (`gate:foundation:verification`). The coverage premises must fail on a missing requirement mapping or unrendered traced decision. These checks establish a reviewable design and its correspondence; the later implementation evidence gate remains (`gate:design:implementation-evidence`).

**Open Question (Secret-consuming integrations)** · `open:design:secret-integrations` Owner: the foundation implementer and the storage and key-adapter implementers, with repository review before each affected integration is enabled. The secrecy choice is settled under (`dec:observe:secrets`); acquisition, parser, driver, exporter and process behavior remain held until (`inv:observe:secret-boundary`) and (`gate:observe:foundation`) have reviewed type/data-flow evidence and adversarial execution claims under (`rule:testing:coverage`). Settlement requires no ordinary retained private copies or printable private fields, including unsanitized dependency errors, together with full recording of public content. The library's Debug implementation alone cannot settle this hold. An incompatible dependency requires a conforming integration or replacement, not a filtering exception. Observation follows (`rec:observe:decision`). The PostgreSQL route is (`dec:storage:secret-shim`); its driver part is settled for the host-secured store transport: acquisition, authentication, handoff, filters, cancellation and diagnostics. The statement and result surface, iteration-bound evidence and relay publication order are admitted for the storage library under (`amend:storage:library-admission`); executable persistence remains disabled until that amendment’s separate enabling conditions hold. The host-intranet cases and public acquisition declaration are (`rec:intranet:decision`); the closed storage vocabulary is covered by the library admission, while TLS admission from (`amend:storage:tls-evaluation`) moves to (`open:intranet:guarded-transport`). All other integration holds are unchanged.

**Amendment (Boundary snapshot representation)** · `amend:design:boundary-snapshot-representation` Implements (`amend:design:bite-one-closure-inputs`) with fixed pre-epoch history and a separate canonical asserted-parent field in the structural body. For candidate admission, both asserted parents and removable declared dependencies resolve against history or proposals strictly earlier in published (epoch, position) placement, independently of attached time; the distinct union of asserted parents and typed settlement references defines closures. Finalized-history replay retains declared-dependency violations as evidence rather than refusing the authoritative sequence; finalized replay retains absent or non-earlier asserted parents as parent-placement faults; candidate formation keeps its refusals. A principal closure contains its root and causal ancestors outside history. Shared history parents add no membership, and dependency tombstones do not obstruct assembly. Closures retain overlap and whole co-legs, and their union equals the proposed act set. A shared visited memo expands each proposal once; recorded work counts node visits, parent inspections, inherited-member insert attempts and emitted members, with the same increments in the separate records assembly_steps counter, while work_operations counts invocations only. A typed exhaustion refusal remains held for the computation budget of (`req:design:limits`); no budget is invented. A late declaration announcement refuses the snapshot under (`verif:design:effective-edition-cases`); bite 2 confirms or explicitly changes that rule.

**Amendment (Provisional carriage bounds)** · `amend:design:provisional-carriage-bounds` The 1024-entry limits on declared dependencies and asserted parents are provisional representation bounds, held for the published count parameters supplied by bite 2’s (`model:design:declarations`). Identifier byte-length bounds are absent and held for that same declaration and host-validation work; these local limits do not discharge (`req:design:limits`). Both lists use derived ActId order: author bytes, then sequence, then family bytes. This structural order stands until the first byte boundary fixes the canonical encoding’s order. The settlement carrier adds settles and Ratify’s accepts to the causal-parent relation through StructuralBody::causal_parents; overlap with the canonical asserted list contributes once, without increasing its authored-list bound.

**Amendment (Bite 1 harness submission)** · `amend:design:bite-one-harness-submission` The harness-evidence slice of (`amend:design:bite-one-step-plan`) supplies the records-dag-1 generator, shared exact-cause conformance and recorded properties through the records public surface in pn-records and pn-verification. The independent naive transitive-search model, reversal checks, whole-leg ownership, work count and monotone extension checks support the internal input contract, not graph partition or ledger validity. The outer consumer uses only development dependencies and the same generator; it is not a second implementation. The complete bite review must examine the generator distribution and shrink behavior, oracle independence, the distinction between history and proposal parents, typed fault premises, observation outcomes and retained performance findings. The membership partition stays owned by pn-graph in bite 3, count/byte limit declarations by the declarations implementer in bite 2, and host admission and ledger claims by their later bites.

**Amendment (Declaration family payload binding)** · `amend:design:declaration-family-binding` Refines (`model:design:declarations`): payload binding is keyed by declaration kind, the namespace and name pair. The numeric/constants family binds its complete set of ConstantsEdition payloads; another pair, including marker-policy declarations, neither requires those numeric payloads nor resolves through that binder. Each later family binds its own payload type, preserving the common announcement and effective-boundary rules without importing unrelated floor, numeraire or controller requirements.

**Amendment (Storage library evidence boundary)** · `amend:design:storage-library-evidence` Under (`open:design:secret-integrations`), the host-secured transport, bounded SCRAM work and relay evidence accompany the closed statement and result witnesses in (`amend:storage:library-conformance`). The independent review with executed evidence is settled by (`amend:storage:library-admission`). Schema and write paths remain confined to the library and service tests until an executable meets that admission’s separate boundary, persistence-enabling and credential-custody conditions. Stored canonical public inputs and their permanent recipes meet this slice's byte-retention and reconstruction duties under (`amend:decomposition:deployment-evidence`). Numeric rung transcripts and admitted epoch results belong to the bites that produce them; this storage slice does not allocate the deployment duties left unplaced by that amendment.

**Amendment (Bite 3 slices by content)** · `amend:design:bite-three-step-plan` Under (`gate:design:bite-3`), the formation slice introduces pn-graph and its inherited workspace petgraph declaration, freezes the family census, endpoint typing and prescribed tensor pipeline, and implements validate-formation, project-complete-act and partition-dependencies through the records public surface. Its witnesses cover complete authorized projections, refusal of partial formation, deterministic reconstruction indices, unrelated live-index stability after removal, parallel projections and revalidation after deserialization. The partition consumes the principal closures of (`amend:design:bite-one-closure-inputs`) against one fixed pre-epoch history and checks disjointness, coverage and processing-order independence with the independent small-case oracle of (`verif:design:closure-partition-cases`); common-marker and host-determination evidence remain separate obligations. The geometry slice implements evaluate-geometry under (`alg:design:geometry`), comparing exact geometry with independently enclosed nonlinear probes and establishing sound-or-indecisive graph comparisons through the existing numeric claim. Freezing the tensor pipeline in formation fixes coordinate roles and structural exclusions, not executed geometry. Observation, local test levels, shared conformance and recorded properties accompany each implemented surface under (`gate:design:harness-bites`), with the warning budgets of (`rule:design:development-budgets`). The harness-evidence close joins every required tier for the same source and supported configuration under (`rule:design:harness-gates`) and records claim evidence and remaining holds under (`open:design:claim-evidence`). Mint the membership partition claim and the scoped formation premises of the formation-title claim only when their tests first establish them; broader title recognition, settlement recognition and folds remain with (`gate:design:bite-4`). Serde graph bytes remain outside canonical publication and binding replay comparison under (`proto:design:encoding`).

**Amendment (Records-level graph partition)** · `amend:design:graph-partition-scope` Under (`amend:design:bite-three-step-plan`), pn-graph consumes the immutable principal closures supplied by records and publishes their disjoint component unions after comparing the result with the proposed whole-act identities. Reconstruction of the temporary UnGraph fixes root and edge order; each intersection weight retains only the least shared act identity. Complete intersections remain recoverable from the records closures. The graph therefore stores at most N(N-1)/2 edge-weight identities for N roots; identifier lengths, closure storage and runtime remain separate costs. The membership claim is minted in this carrier scope, while (`verif:design:closure-partition-cases`) remains held for common final-marker, target/minimum, maximum-duration and host-determination evidence. Family formation, projection validation, authenticated approval/removal, StableDiGraph storage and revalidation, geometry and title remain unimplemented. The implemented partition does not certify those prerequisites or any canonical graph byte encoding.

**Amendment (Finalized records replay)** · `amend:design:finalized-records-replay` Under network-v0.31.1, ``post:graph:ordering-accountability``, finalized records replay preserves every published act and stamp while collecting disclosed dependency violations and public causal-parent placement faults. Missing, self and later parents do not refuse replay or enter the recomputation maximum. Candidate admission still refuses them. Under network-v0.31.1, ``def:graph:authoritative-act-order`` and ``def:graph:title-certificate``, each epoch’s complete written set must publish exactly positions 1 to the authoritative sequence’s cardinality, each once. Candidate validation checks fixed history together with proposals; finalized replay checks the supplied history. RepeatedPosition refuses a repeated key; after duplicate detection, the first sorted mismatch reports MissingPosition for an internal gap within the epoch size, or PositionOutsideEpoch for a position above that size. These diagnoses can overlap mathematically: an out-of-range member also leaves a gap, and the stated precedence supplies one deterministic cause. Epoch-zero and position-zero retain FinalizationEpoch and FinalizedPosition. An absent order is malformed certificate input, refused before time or host-fault replay, not a retained ordering fault. Noncanonical parent lists remain structural refusals. Full and FullEmpty dependency lists support recomputation; a tombstone and its dependent maxima carry explicit unavailable evidence under (`open:design:dependency-opening-recipe`). No host fault changes identity, order, projection, count, debit, standing or title.

**Open Question (Finalized-time implementation evidence)** · `open:design:noncompliant-logical-time` Implementation evidence is closed for carried Full and FullEmpty dependency projections under network-v0.31.1, ``def:graph:act-incidence-causality``, ``prop:graph:act-time-finite``, ``def:graph:parent-placement-fault``, ``def:graph:stamp-fault`` and ``post:graph:ordering-accountability``. Replay recomputes using earlier finalized predecessors and incident frontiers carried across epochs, excludes absent and misplaced predecessors from maxima, and retains public stamps and parent-placement faults without rewriting finalized acts. ``claim:verification:carried-time-replay-matches-paper-fixture`` compares all {{ numval('num:numbers:released-time-fixture-field-count') }} released fixture fields; ``claim:acts:time-evidence-never-rewrites-finalization`` witnesses unavailable propagation and the distinction between a concealed empty list and a concealed dependency on time five. Tombstoned projections and their affected successors remain unavailable under (`open:design:dependency-opening-recipe`); this is conservative replay evidence, not an undefined time rule or an impossibility claim. Unavailable means not established by this replay, not unestablishable from retained data. This conservative replay does not compute interval bounds, dominance proofs or out-of-interval stamp faults: even a tombstoned first act and a successor whose available frontier dominates the concealed predecessor remain unavailable. Interval reasoning is a separate evidence enhancement; authenticated reopening remains the recipe obligation. Neither limitation bounds recognition or title. Recognition retains stamp evidence without using it as a premise; absent settlement targets fail clause (ii), ``itm:graph:settlement-recognition-reference-binding``, and misplaced targets fail clause (vi), ``itm:graph:settlement-recognition-well-formedness``. ``claim:verification:settlement-replay-retains-target-placement-faults`` witnesses every clause in the isolated absent and misplaced cases, including the additional failures specified by (`alg:design:recognition`), with candidate formation still refusing both. Finalized title replay and its scoped close are established in (`amend:design:bite-four-close`); the dependency-opening recipe remains an evidence limitation, never a title prerequisite.

**Open Question (Finalized-order implementation evidence)** · `open:design:cross-epoch-order` Implementation evidence is closed under network-v0.31.1, ``def:graph:authoritative-act-order``, ``rem:graph:order-of-record-is-position`` and ``rem:graph:cross-epoch-precedence``. Candidate checks, replay, reconstruction and recognition compare epoch first and finalized position second; published logical time never participates. ``claim:acts:epoch-qualified-placement-retains-causal-checks`` witnesses cross-epoch placement and the malformed-order refusals now stated by ``def:graph:title-certificate``, and ``claim:verification:carried-time-replay-matches-paper-fixture`` witnesses a later disjoint act with lower time, cross-epoch frontier carry and retained placement faults. Under network-v0.31.1, ``def:graph:authoritative-act-order`` and ``def:graph:title-certificate``, each epoch’s complete written set must publish exactly positions 1 to the authoritative sequence’s cardinality, each once. Candidate validation checks fixed history together with proposals; finalized replay checks the supplied history. RepeatedPosition refuses a repeated key; after duplicate detection, the first sorted mismatch reports MissingPosition for an internal gap within the epoch size, or PositionOutsideEpoch for a position above that size. These diagnoses can overlap mathematically: an out-of-range member also leaves a gap, and the stated precedence supplies one deterministic cause. Epoch-zero and position-zero retain FinalizationEpoch and FinalizedPosition. An absent order is malformed certificate input, refused before time or host-fault replay, not a retained ordering fault. No paper or implementation ordering choice remains held here.

**Amendment (Scoped Registration projection)** · `amend:design:registration-formation-scope` Under (`amend:design:bite-three-step-plan`), pn-graph now projects and reconstructs only complete Registration acts through a declared public approval/removal verifier, retaining the existing Actor/Profile pair, every parallel binary edge and inherited finalization metadata. The direct StableDiGraph value is disposable; mutation and diagnostic serde decoding require typed revalidation against complete public history. The first geometry or title consumer must take that history and the public verifier and call validate_registration_graph itself before using a RegistrationGraph; a caller assertion of prior validation is insufficient. Mixed-family consumers likewise call validate_formation. This is a consumer contract, not a wrapper token or an implemented consumer. Removal authorization never deletes structural projections. Local public and observation witnesses establish this conditional structural scope; cryptographic realization, shared Registration conformance, recorded properties, the remaining family census and tensor pipeline, geometry and title remain held. The later records changes close published-order and carried-list time correspondence in (`open:design:noncompliant-logical-time`) and (`open:design:cross-epoch-order`); tombstone authentication remains under (`open:design:dependency-opening-recipe`).

**Amendment (Census and binary formation extension)** · `amend:design:affinity-formation-scope` Under (`amend:design:bite-three-step-plan`), the graph dataset now freezes all nineteen authored families and twenty-seven projection rows, with the endpoint taxonomy and five-stage tensor pipeline documented separately from executable geometry. Registration and Affinity share reconstruction, authorized opening-removal checks and typed revalidation; only these two families are implemented. Affinity's exact bounded rational carrier is an internal refinement of records quantities, not canonical publication. Coordinate's derived equality is structural: 1/2 differs from 2/4, as required for exact authored-carrier revalidation. The first geometry consumer must add an explicit rational-value comparison and state whether each comparison uses structural or value equality; no value comparison is implemented here. Every remaining family, general minted-node and mandatory-reference validation, shared conformance and recorded properties remain held. These local formation witnesses are supplemented by the later records evidence in (`open:design:noncompliant-logical-time`) and (`open:design:cross-epoch-order`); tombstone authentication remains under (`open:design:dependency-opening-recipe`).

**Amendment (Minted reference carrier)** · `amend:design:minted-reference-carrier` Extends (`amend:design:bite-one-internal-envelopes`) with the distinct records Minted(ActId) reference required by network-v0.31.1, ``def:graph:identifier-algebra`` and ``def:graph:act-identifier``. Complete self-minting terminal targets in Publish, Owner, Participant, Review, Send and Bid derive node references alongside act and edge identities; ordinary acts do not mint. The existing act identifier is finite before finalization, so a genesis act can carry its own minted target without circular identity. Records validate and resolve this structural reference, while pn-graph owns complete family incidence, endpoint typing and authenticated approval. This carrier adds no codec or canonical byte profile; the first byte boundary remains held.

**Amendment (Publish formation scope)** · `amend:design:publish-formation-scope` Extends (`amend:design:affinity-formation-scope`) with scoped Publish binary projections over typed minted Content terms. Exact attachment and the immutable genesis-only attribution/provenance pair pass through the existing public approval/removal boundary and shared deterministic reconstruction and typed revalidation. Genesis self-minting and ordinary publication remain distinct without re-minting an existing target; syntactically dangling incidence establishes no anchor. The carrier is an internal refinement, not canonical publication or license-policy execution. Registration, Affinity and Publish are implemented; the other {{ numval('num:numbers:publish-stage-unimplemented-family-count') }} families, general reference conformance, shared formation conformance, recorded properties, geometry and title remain held under (`open:design:claim-evidence`). The time and order implementation obligations remain held.

**Amendment (Owner formation scope)** · `amend:design:owner-formation-scope` Extends (`amend:design:publish-formation-scope`) with Owner through the same binary reconstruction, removal authorization and typed revalidation. Under network-v0.31.1, ``edge:nodes:owner`` and ``def:graph:item-genesis-act``, typed Item identity retains the genesis act identifier while ordinary Owner projections mint nothing and confer no title force. The scoped carrier checks signed attachment and refuses license pairs and protocol references; no title fold or geometry is implemented. Owner precedes Bid because Bid requires Item endpoints. Participant, Review, Send and Bid remain subsequent slices, together with the other unimplemented families and general reference and shared formation conformance. The time and order implementation obligations and broader evidence remain held under (`open:design:claim-evidence`).

**Amendment (Participant formation scope)** · `amend:design:participant-formation-scope` Extends (`amend:design:owner-formation-scope`) with the first complete paired family under network-v0.31.1, ``edge:nodes:participant``. The existing graph entry points return a complete ordered projection vector and use one reconstruction path for binary and paired acts, materializing endpoint nodes before their edges. Participant introduces typed Chat for later Send formation, checking Actor-to-Chat-to-Chat incidence and bounded interactivity, responsibility and continuity while retaining one act identity and attached time on both roles. Found, join, found successor and move differ only by endpoint terms; formation asserts no origin membership, standing or title. The five implemented families are Registration, Affinity, Publish, Owner and Participant; the other {{ numval('num:numbers:participant-stage-unimplemented-family-count') }}, general mandatory-reference conformance, broader shared properties, geometry and title remain held under (`open:design:claim-evidence`). The time and order implementation obligations remain held.

**Amendment (Review formation scope)** · `amend:design:review-formation-scope` Under (`amend:design:bite-three-step-plan`), Review follows Participant because its complete paired projection machinery and minted identities are available. Review adds all remaining passive constructors for the universal Reviewable middle and forms author-fixed A/T bundles through the same reconstruction, approval/removal authorization and typed revalidation used by the five earlier families. Its terminal remains Comment, its two signed coordinates retain authored structural equality, and both legs share one attached time. Source lookup, endpoint representability and anchoring remain distinct under (`alg:design:formation`). Send and Bid formation remain subsequent slices despite their node constructors now being representable. The time and order implementation obligations, geometry, title and broader shared conformance remain held.

**Amendment (Send formation scope)** · `amend:design:send-formation-scope` Under (`amend:design:bite-three-step-plan`), Send follows Review because typed Chat and Message constructors and complete paired reconstruction are available. One signed importance coordinate and shared act metadata survive the A/T projection, authorization, deterministic reconstruction and typed revalidation path. The family has no license pair or protocol references. Its frozen role assignment follows network-v0.31.1, ``edge:nodes:hyper-send``; geometry, title, the remaining families and the time and order implementation obligations remain held.

**Amendment (Bid formation scope)** · `amend:design:bid-formation-scope` Under (`amend:design:bite-three-step-plan`), Bid follows Send with typed Item and Offer constructors and shared paired reconstruction already available. The fresh terminal must name the Bid act itself, so no ordinary Bid can reuse an Offer; this is the family-specific freshness rule of network-v0.31.1, ``edge:nodes:hyper-bid``. Signed generosity and nonnegative urgency retain their complete shared tuple, including zero values, through approval, authorized removal and typed revalidation. No license pair or protocol reference is admitted. Settlement recognition, title, geometry, the remaining families and the time and order implementation obligations remain held.

**Amendment (Opinion formation scope)** · `amend:design:opinion-formation-scope` Under (`amend:design:bite-three-step-plan`), Opinion follows Bid because every passive constructor and shared binary reconstruction are available. Signed polarity and reaction retain their exact authored carriers alongside identity and finalization metadata. Approval, permitted opening removal and typed revalidation share the existing path; malformed or unsupported acts refuse the whole result. The family implements structural incidence from network-v0.31.1, ``edge:nodes:opinion``, without sentiment folds, standing allocation or new ordering assumptions. Ten families and general conformance remain to be formed; geometry, title and the time and order implementation obligations remain held.

**Amendment (Join Request formation scope)** · `amend:design:join-request-formation-scope` Under (`amend:design:bite-three-step-plan`), Join Request follows Opinion because the typed Chat constructor, exact binary coordinate carrier and shared reconstruction already exist. Its two signed coordinates retain their authored structure and finalization metadata through complete approval, authorized removal and revalidation. The proposal from network-v0.31.1, ``edge:nodes:join-request``, creates no membership or Chat-state change. Nine families and general conformance remain; geometry, title and the time and order implementation obligations stay held without new ordering assumptions.

**Amendment (Tag formation scope)** · `amend:design:tag-formation-scope` Under (`amend:design:bite-three-step-plan`), Tag follows Join Request with every permitted passive middle constructor and the public Type terminal already available. The seven-member Taggable census excludes Offer, as network-v0.31.1, ``tbl:nodes:affordance-traits``, requires. Shared reconstruction retains complete paired relevance/confidence tuples, exact identity and metadata, approvals and authorized removal; typed revalidation refuses changed or partial bundles. Classification mints nothing and gives no standing verdict. Eight families and general conformance remain, with geometry, title and the time and order implementation obligations held.

**Amendment (Leave formation scope)** · `amend:design:leave-formation-scope` Under (`amend:design:bite-three-step-plan`), Leave follows Tag because typed Chat, binary reconstruction and authorization are available without a settlement-reference carrier. Its fixed control parameters require no authored quantities. The unconditional departure of network-v0.31.1, ``edge:nodes:leave``, adds no membership prerequisite and computes no fold. Invitation and De-invite use Actor-to-Chat-to-Profile incidence; Withdraw and Rescind use Actor-to-Offer incidence; Accept and Ratify use Actor-to-Actor incidence with settlement act references. None requires Reference's disputed endpoints. Those six families, Reference, general mandatory-reference and shared conformance, geometry and title remain unimplemented. The time and order implementation obligations stay held without new ordering assumptions.

**Amendment (Formed reference conformance)** · `amend:design:formed-reference-conformance` Under (`alg:design:formation`), records resolution and graph incidence remain separate obligations. The twelve implemented family blocks define no protocol-reference slots, including network-v0.31.1, ``edge:nodes:registration`` and ``edge:nodes:affinity``; resolved extra references therefore refuse formation rather than silently acquiring a meaning. Scoped conformance checks that rule across all twelve families and compares all six formed genesis terms with records resolution before and after authorized payload removal. Dangling Opinion incidence is admitted for all six minted constructors, including Item, under (`alg:design:formation`); it establishes no genesis anchor. Accept and Ratify's required references, the other unimplemented families and complete shared conformance remain held under (`open:design:claim-evidence`); neither ordering question gains a verdict.

**Amendment (Reference formation scope)** · `amend:design:reference-formation-scope` Reference follows Leave under (`amend:design:bite-three-step-plan`) using the eight passive constructors already carried by Opinion. The normative network-v0.31.1 block ``edge:nodes:hyper-reference`` fixes Actor-to-Artifact A incidence and Artifact-to-passive-Target T incidence; A lands on the citing artifact, which sources only the matched author-fixed T leg (portion{``lem:graph:passive-out-degree``; Reference incidence with A landing on the citing artifact and that artifact sourcing the matched author-fixed T leg}). The edge block governs this formation contract. Shared reconstruction, approval, authorized removal, typed revalidation and whole-result refusal apply to Reference. No standing allocation, tensor evaluation or title fold is established; the time and order implementation obligations remain held.

**Amendment (Invitation formation scope)** · `amend:design:invitation-formation-scope` Invitation follows Reference because typed Chat and Profile endpoints and the exact three-coordinate carrier are available. Its complete Actor-to-Chat-to-Profile tuple follows network-v0.31.1, ``edge:nodes:hyper-invitation``, through shared reconstruction and revalidation. Formation does not recognize participation or compile standing. The consumer revalidation contract, whole-result refusal and the time and order implementation obligations are unchanged.

**Amendment (De-invite formation scope)** · `amend:design:deinvite-formation-scope` De-invite follows Invitation because it uses the same Chat-to-Profile paired incidence with no authored coordinates. The unconditional control contract of network-v0.31.1, ``edge:nodes:hyper-deinvite``, adds no prior Invitation or membership prerequisite. Shared complete reconstruction and typed revalidation retain both roles; membership policy and standing compilation remain later consumers; De-invite changes no standing quantity and resolves home at coefficient one under ``rem:epoch:control-acts-resolve-to-self``.

**Amendment (Withdraw formation scope)** · `amend:design:withdraw-formation-scope` Withdraw follows the Chat control families because Bid already supplies the typed Offer constructor. Its binary control incidence follows network-v0.31.1, ``edge:nodes:withdraw``, through shared whole-result reconstruction and revalidation. This does not decide buyer consent, settlement state or cancellation precedence; those remain recognition obligations. No parent-refusal or logical-time availability premise is added.

**Amendment (Rescind formation scope)** · `amend:design:rescind-formation-scope` Rescind follows Withdraw with the same typed Actor-to-Offer binary incidence and a distinct family projection under network-v0.31.1, ``edge:nodes:rescind``. Shared authorization, reconstruction and structural revalidation preserve the control record without evaluating seller consent or settlement state. Accept and Ratify's mandatory act references are the remaining formation work; the time and order implementation obligations remain held.

**Amendment (Seventeen-family formation conformance)** · `amend:design:seventeen-family-conformance` Reference, Invitation, De-invite, Withdraw and Rescind extend the formed set to seventeen of the nineteen authored families under (`amend:design:bite-three-step-plan`). Shared conformance now checks complete authorization and revalidation for all seventeen in one history. Accept and Ratify remain with their mandatory settlement-reference integration: network-v0.31.1, ``def:graph:settlement-act-reference``, makes the Bid-to-Accept-to-Ratify references causal parents, while the current records closure consumes only the explicit asserted-parent carrier. This slice does not add a duplicate-declaration requirement or a new dependence on replay refusing parent placement. Resolving that carrier integration, the time and order implementation obligations, geometry and the harness-evidence close remain; title recognition and folds stay in bite 4.

**Amendment (Settlement causal-parent integration)** · `amend:design:settlement-parent-integration` The records carrier closes the explicit-parent gap identified in (`amend:design:seventeen-family-conformance`) under network-v0.31.1, ``def:graph:settlement-act-reference`` and ``def:graph:act-causal-parents``. Accept and Ratify use different typed variants; all other families refuse the field. The canonical asserted list and the settlement targets form one distinct parent set, so an author need not repeat metadata and an explicit repetition changes neither closure membership nor counted assembly work. Resolution checks target existence and the existing parent placement rules. Earlier wrong-family targets and a Ratify naming a different Bid remain authored records; recognition clause (ii) checks Bid genesis identity and the exact Accept’s matching Bid. No graph edge points to the Offer through this metadata. Records supplies local resolution; graph formation must still form and authorize the referenced acts regardless of their asserted settlement role. The structural carrier has no new byte profile or digest. The paper time and cross-epoch rules at network-v0.31.1 are implemented for carried lists, with evidence in (`open:design:noncompliant-logical-time`) and (`open:design:cross-epoch-order`); tombstone authentication remains under (`open:design:dependency-opening-recipe`). Finalized replay must retain absent or misplaced settlement-parent faults without refusal, while formation still refuses them; recognition records the complete six-clause maps in (`alg:design:recognition`), including absent-target (i), (ii), (iii), (vi) and misplaced-target (vi) failures where their premises fail.

**Amendment (Settlement formation and complete census)** · `amend:design:settlement-formation-scope` Accept follows the typed records carrier and existing Bid formation; Ratify follows Accept in the valid binding chain; a wrong-family reference still requires its target's complete authorized projection. Under network-v0.31.1, ``edge:nodes:accept``, ``edge:nodes:ratify`` and ``def:graph:settlement-act-reference``, both retain Actor-to-Actor incidence and signed comfort/equity, with typed settlement metadata rather than an Offer endpoint. project_complete_act receives the validated history and requires the exact act to belong to it; the shared formation path checks complete referenced-act projections, author approval and permitted removals before returning a settlement projection. For a binding candidate the Bid author identifies the buyer, the Accept author identifies the consenting seller, and Ratify reverses those Actor endpoints; formation retains the authored Actor endpoints even when references do not bind. This closes the carrier integration held by (`amend:design:seventeen-family-conformance`) and extends shared whole-result conformance to all nineteen authored families and twenty-seven projections. The narrow complete-authorization claim supplies formation premises only: The planned ``claim:formation:recognized-title-follows-complete-authorized-acts`` is narrowed by the scoped recognition/title close in (`amend:design:bite-four-close`); deployment authentication remains under (`open:design:claim-evidence`). Geometry and the harness-evidence close remain held. The later records implementation closes the carried-list time and published-order obligations; authenticated tombstone openings remain under (`open:design:dependency-opening-recipe`).

