**Algorithm (Reference inverse-gain wall)** · `alg:design:reference-wall` The separate reference evaluator under (`sig:design:numeric-kernel`) retains the defining target w*: G(w*) = 1/(2 mu S_char W), with squared bleed {{ numval('num:numbers:reference-squared-bleed') }}. For W > 0, existence and uniqueness follow from the positive, continuous, strictly decreasing deployed gain and its endpoint limits in network-v0.31.1, ``prop:epoch:wall-controller-total``; finite tests do not establish that premise. The declaration supplies a positive rational initial bracket [a,b], a positive rational stopping width, an increasing absolute-width precision sequence under (`rule:design:refinement`), an iteration cap and separate production and replay Work allowances. Before scalar evaluation, require K >= 2, K < 64 and a >= 1/(2^K - 2) for the single declared reduction ceiling K. Evaluate G(a) - t and G(b) - t outward, in that order, where t = 1/(2 mu S_char W), and require respectively strict positive and strict negative signs. A separated wrong sign refuses the bracket. At each step evaluate the dyadic midpoint (a+b)/2 outward; a positive sign replaces a and a negative sign replaces b. An unresolved sign runs the complete declared precision sequence, then stops INDECISIVE without guessing or shrinking the bracket. Operational exhaustion is distinct from a mathematical sign. Stop only when b-a is at most the declared width; the result [a_k,b_k] encloses w*, never replaces it by a point. Retain every attempted query, precision, checked attachment, sign and Work charge, including endpoint checks and failed refinements. Independent replay rebuilds every query from the declared inputs and earlier checked signs before checking the attachment; supplied queries and bounds are not authority.

