**Amendment (Sibling response outside selected paths)** · `amend:design:sibling-outside-paths` Under ``prop:dynamics:sibling-response-without-path``, ``claim:standing:sibling-response-refuses-selected-path-independence`` checks a decreasing i response to j with the only non-self base allocations in the positive u→i and u→j cells and absorbing i and j. The matched complete histories change only j balance, taking its rate from {{ numval('num:numbers:partition-low-rate') }} to {{ numval('num:numbers:sibling-raised-rate') }}, while u retains {{ numval('num:numbers:partition-high-rate') }} and i retains {{ numval('num:numbers:partition-low-rate') }}. Emission is saturated and the entire j hull is above the structural wall. The declared precision is {{ numval('num:numbers:sibling-precision') }} bits and the proposal grid is {{ numval('num:numbers:sibling-endpoint-grid') }}. Both certified solves select the positive passing strength {{ numval('num:numbers:sibling-strength') }} and independently replay; i decreases with response lower {{ numrel('num:numbers:sibling-response-lower') }} and upper {{ numrel('num:numbers:sibling-response-upper') }}, although j does not contribute pair mass to i. Additional replayed maps varying only j certify a decreasing i image. The positive first-hop exponent, strictly ordered source rates, positive allocations and above-wall sibling instantiate the proposition negative derivative; finite differences are not substituted for differentiation. A selected direct u→i path reads unchanged inputs, so its independence cannot imply conserved-map independence or entrywise derivative domination. The narrow mint is ``claim:standing:sibling-response-refuses-selected-path-independence``. This is a counterexample to a selected-transport-path inference, not a claim that effects propagate without a dependency path. A separate control removes u→j: j is then absent from every row in the closed u,i subsystem and its own row is absorbing. This compiled structure