**Algorithm (Effective schedules)** · `alg:design:effective-schedules` The closed canonical controller edition and every initialization field enter through (`rule:design:wall-delivery-declaration`). An initial exact wall-price pair and policy precede the first completed epoch. Each boundary request names a frozen compiled completed epoch, its declared inverse search, the announcement boundary and the immediately following effective boundary, plus an optional next policy. The completed marker and charged pair must match the current effective pair, and the next package must retain every earlier binding unchanged, designing the in-epoch binding of ``cond:epoch:safety-in-epoch-certification``. A same-boundary announcement, skipped boundary or changed historical prefix is a schedule-validity refusal under ``cond:epoch:safety-publication-lead``. The controller reads the completed width and accepted rung; no next-width forecast, boundary demand or admission statistics are accepted (``cond:epoch:safety-input-quarantine``). A missing policy announcement retains the preceding policy; an announced policy below the led wall refuses at publication (``def:epoch:policy-floor``) (``eq:epoch:floor-validity``). The exact charge is the delivered rational wall times the declared numeraire, checked against the independently replayed controller burn image, designing the finite sequence check of ``cond:epoch:safety-derivability``. Each publication carries its completed marker, effective pair, numeraire, policy and controller proposal with retained inputs, fence and burn image (``cond:epoch:safety-controller-publication``). The sequence record carries the initialization declaration with its edition and constants once. Only independent sequence replay constructs CheckedSchedule; it grants no write admission, persistent application or deployment authentication. A retained policy is checked again at every led wall; a rising wall that overtakes it refuses ScheduleValidity even though the policy was valid at the preceding boundary.

