ing}; accepted-fixture timings alone do not establish either complete condition. The profile rows state the remaining premises separately, rather than a generic calibration hold. {% for item in host_profile if item.name == 'maximum-replay-work' %}The measured solve-replay maximum is {{ item.number_forms.measured_maximum | display }} Work; the declared fixture-only margin is {{ item.number_forms.margin_multiple | display }} times that maximum, giving {{ item.number_forms.measured_envelope | display }} Work. Its scope is {{ item.measurement_scope }}. This remains a pending live setting, not a completion promise or a gate budget.{% endfor %} {% for item in host_profile if item.name == 'validation-timeout' %}The warm semantic-consumption maximum is {{ item.number_forms.measured_validation_maximum_ns | display }} nanoseconds; its fixture-only margin is {{ item.number_forms.margin_multiple | display }} times the maximum, or {{ item.number_forms.measured_envelope_ns | display }} nanoseconds. This excludes complete host validation and supplies no protocol timeout.{% endfor %} The deployment selects its fixed floor only after the comparison required by ``cond:deployment:wall-calibration``. The supported numerical platform is (`rule:design:backend-platform-admission`); complete service-platform support remains pending its integration evidence. Protocol timeouts never reuse per-commit gate budgets. X1 through X9, publication lead, input quarantine and no-write-on-indecision remain binding. Monitoring under ``post:deployment:centralized-monitoring`` records sampling, thresholds, persistence and review disposition, not a hidden controller input. Lane/graph owns epoch-to-publication semantics, marker verification and numerical context; lane/design owns transport, storage, wallet, API, client and commands. Each package has one writer at a time.

